01  The Program


Here's the whole thing before it gets taken apart. It's deliberately small — one malloc, one loop, one infinite wait — so that every line can be tied back to a specific OS concept.
MEMORY.C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    int mb;

    if (argc != 2)
    {
        printf("Usage: %s <memory_in_MB>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    mb = atoi(argv[1]);

    char *memory = malloc(mb * 1024 * 1024);

    if (memory == NULL)
    {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < mb * 1024 * 1024; i += 4096)
    {
        memory[i] = 1;
    }

    printf("%d MB memory allocated.\n", mb);
    printf("Program is running... Press Ctrl+C to exit.\n");

    while (1)
    {
        sleep(1);
    }

    return 0;
}

02  Compiling and Running


Two commands are all this needs: compile it, then run it with a number.
COMPILE
gcc memory.c -o memory

RUN — ALLOCATE 500 MB
./memory 500

Here ./memory is the program itself, and 500 is the size in megabytes — it becomes argv[1] inside the program.


While it's running, open a second terminal and watch the memory usage change:
free -h
watch -n 1 free -h

WHY A SECOND TERMINAL


The program blocks forever in its own terminal (that's the point — it needs to keep holding the memory), so a separate window is needed to observe the system's memory counters while it runs.
