04  argc and argv


The task specifically calls for taking the memory size as a command-line argument, which is why main is declared with two parameters instead of none:
int main(int argc, char *argv[])




argc — argument count — is how many words were typed on the command line, including the program's own name. argv — argument vector — is where those words are actually stored, as an array of strings.
Command line
argc
argv[0]
argv[1]
./memory 500
2
"./memory"
"500" (a string)


Note that argv[1] holds the text "500" — a string of two characters, not the number 500. That distinction is exactly what section 6 deals with.
05  Checking the Number of Arguments


Before trusting argv[1], the program makes sure it actually exists:
if (argc != 2)
{
    printf("Usage: %s <memory_in_MB>\n", argv[0]);
    return 1;
}

Exactly one argument is expected from the user, which means argc should equal 2 — the program name plus the size. Running ./memory 500 gives argc = 2, so this check passes silently and execution continues.


Running the program with nothing after it —
./memory

— leaves argc = 1. The condition argc != 2 is now true, so the program prints a usage message and exits with status 1 instead of trying to read an argument that was never given.
06  Getting the Memory Size
mb = atoi(argv[1]);




argv[1] is the string "500" — perfectly readable to a human, but useless in arithmetic as far as the compiler is concerned. atoi() ("ASCII to integer") converts it into the actual number 500, which is what gets stored in mb and used for every calculation from here on.
07  Allocating Memory


This is the line the whole exercise is built around:
char *memory = malloc(mb * 1024 * 1024);




With mb = 500, the expression 500 × 1024 × 1024 works out to roughly 524,288,000 bytes — 500 mebibytes, requested from the heap in one call.
Why char *?
memory is declared as a pointer, because that's what malloc() actually gives back: not the memory itself, but the address of where it starts. char is used because it's exactly one byte wide, which makes indexing into the block — memory[i] — line up neatly with individual bytes.
MEMORY HOLDS THE STARTING ADDRESS
memory
  |
  v
+------+------+------+------+-----+
| [0]  | [1]  | [2]  | [3]  | ... |
+------+------+------+------+-----+
 <------  500 x 1024 x 1024 bytes  ------>

08  Checking Whether Allocation Succeeded
if (memory == NULL)
{
    printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
    return 1;
}




If the system can't satisfy the request — for instance, if 500 MB genuinely isn't available — malloc() signals that by returning NULL instead of a real address. Checking for that before using memory is what keeps a failed allocation from crashing the program later with an invalid pointer.
09  Actually Using the Memory
for (int i = 0; i < mb * 1024 * 1024; i += 4096)
{
    memory[i] = 1;
}




This loop is the part that makes the demonstration actually work. malloc() reserves virtual address space, but Linux doesn't necessarily back all of it with physical RAM right away — a page can stay unmapped until something actually reads or writes it. Without this loop, free -h might not show the full allocation as used.
The loop steps through the block 4096 bytes at a time, because 4 KB is the standard page size on Linux. Writing one byte per page is enough to force that entire page to become resident — writing to every byte would just do the same work far more slowly.
REQUESTING VS. BACKING MEMORY
virtual pages (requested):   [page0] [page1] [page2] [page3] ...
                                |       |       |       |
                          memory[i]=1 touches one byte per page
                                v       v       v       v
resident in physical RAM:    [page0] [page1] [page2] [page3] ...

THE CORE OS CONCEPT


This is the whole point of the lab: a successful malloc() only promises virtual address space. Whether that space is backed by real, physical pages is a separate question — one this loop answers by force.
10  Displaying the Result
printf("%d MB memory allocated.\n", mb);




Running ./memory 500 prints:
500 MB memory allocated.

11  Keeping the Program Running
printf("Program is running... Press Ctrl+C to exit.\n");

while (1)
{
    sleep(1);
}




while (1) is a loop whose condition is always true, so it never ends on its own — it keeps the program alive indefinitely. Inside it, sleep(1) pauses for one second per iteration rather than spinning a CPU core at full speed for no reason. Together they hold the allocated memory open long enough to inspect it with free -h in another terminal.
12  Why free(memory) Is Never Reached
free(memory);




This line sits right after the while (1) loop — but since that loop never exits by itself, execution never falls through to it in normal use. The only way to stop the program is Ctrl+C, which terminates the process outright; the OS then reclaims all of its memory automatically as part of cleaning up.
A more polished version would catch the interrupt signal and call free() explicitly, but for this experiment it isn't necessary — the operating system's own cleanup does the job. Section 18 builds exactly that version.
