TERMINOLOGY NOTE


Strictly, 1024 x 1024 bytes is a mebibyte (MiB), not a megabyte (MB) in the SI sense — but lab exercises and everyday usage almost always call it "MB" anyway, so the program's messages follow that convention.
The experiment, in one sentence: requesting virtual memory and actually using it are two different steps, and this program exists to make that difference visible in free -h.
14  Converting Megabytes to Bytes


Before calling malloc(), the requested size needs to move from megabytes to bytes — the unit malloc() actually works in:
size_t size = (size_t)mb * 1024 * 1024;

mb holds a human-friendly number like 500. Multiplying it by 1024 twice converts megabytes to kilobytes and then to bytes, giving the true byte count malloc() needs. The result is stored in a size_t — an unsigned integer type wide enough to hold a size in bytes, including sizes larger than a plain int can safely represent.
MB TO BYTES
mb            x 1024        x 1024           size
500   -------------------------------->   524,288,000
(int)          (KB)          (bytes)        (size_t)

From here on, malloc(size) replaces malloc(mb * 1024 * 1024) — the same value, just computed once and given a name.
15  The malloc() Call, Revisited


With size computed, the allocation itself is just:
char *memory = malloc(size);

malloc(size) asks the allocator for that many bytes and, on success, returns the address of the first one. char *memory is a pointer wide enough to hold that address — it does not hold the memory itself, only where the memory starts.


What a Box Represents
It's tempting to picture the allocated block as a row of boxes, each one byte wide — and for a char *, that's exactly right, because char is defined to be one byte. But the box size actually comes from the pointer's type, not from a fixed rule:
char * -> [1 B][1 B][1 B][1 B] ...
int  * -> [4 B][4 B][4 B][4 B] ...

Because this program uses char *, it's safe to think of the allocated memory as one giant array of single-byte boxes, and memory[i] as reaching directly into box i.
IF ALLOCATION FAILS


malloc() can't always satisfy a request. When it can't, it returns NULL instead of a real address — which is exactly what the next check is for.
16  Reporting a Failed Allocation
if (memory == NULL)
{
    printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
    return 1;
}

printf() here only reports the problem — it doesn't fix it. The \n moves the terminal to a new line afterward, and the return 1; that follows is what actually ends the program, since continuing with a NULL pointer would crash the moment memory[i] was touched.
FAILURE PATH
malloc(size)
    |
    v
allocation failed?
    |
   yes
    |
    v
print "Memory allocation failed."
    |
    v
return 1  (exit)

17  Virtual Pages and Physical RAM, Per Process


Touching one byte per 4096-byte page, as section 9 covers, tells the operating system that page is actually in use. Extending that idea: a virtual page belonging to one process and a virtual page belonging to another can map to completely different physical RAM frames, even if both processes think of their memory as starting at similar-looking addresses.
Process A                     Process B

Virtual Page 1                Virtual Page 1
      |                              |
      v                              v
RAM Frame A                    RAM Frame D




Nothing here is permanent, though. Under memory pressure the OS can reclaim, evict, or swap pages regardless of whether a process is technically still "holding" them, and calling free() tells the allocator that virtual range is available for reuse. The safe mental model: a process owns virtual pages, and the OS makes sure real memory backs them when the process touches them — not that touching a page grants permanent ownership of one physical RAM page.
18  Releasing Memory on Ctrl+C


Section 12 pointed out that free(memory) is never reached, because the only way to stop the program is Ctrl+C, which the OS handles by tearing the whole process down. It's possible to do better: catching the SIGINT signal lets the program call free() itself before exiting.
Step 1 — Add a header
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>




signal.h provides the signal() function used below to register a handler.
Step 2 — Make the pointer global
A signal handler runs outside main() and takes no arguments of its own, so it needs another way to reach memory. Moving the declaration above main() makes that possible:
char *memory;

Inside main(), the earlier declaration simply drops the type name and assigns the already-global pointer instead: memory = malloc(size);


Step 3 — Write the handler
void handle_sigint(int sig)
{
    free(memory);
    printf("\nMemory released.\n");
    exit(0);
}




This function runs whenever the process receives SIGINT — which is exactly what Ctrl+C sends.
SIGNAL FLOW
Ctrl+C
  |
  v
SIGINT
  |
  v
handle_sigint()
  |
  v
free(memory)
  |
  v
exit(0)




Step 4 — Register it
Near the top of main(), before the allocation:
signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint);




This tells the operating system: when this process receives SIGINT, call handle_sigint() instead of the default action of terminating immediately.
The Updated Program
MEMORY.C — WITH SIGNAL HANDLING
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <signal.h>

char *memory;

void handle_sigint(int sig)
{
    free(memory);
    printf("\nMemory released.\n");
    exit(0);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    int mb;

    if (argc != 2)
    {
        printf("Usage: %s <memory_in_MB>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint);

    mb = atoi(argv[1]);

    size_t size = (size_t)mb * 1024 * 1024;

    memory = malloc(size);

    if (memory == NULL)
    {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < mb * 1024 * 1024; i += 4096)
    {
        memory[i] = 1;
    }

    printf("Memory allocated successfully.\n");
    printf("Press Ctrl+C to release memory and exit.\n");

    while (1)
    {
        sleep(1);
    }

    return 0;
}




Running ./memory 500 and then pressing Ctrl+C now goes through a different ending than before:
UPDATED CLEANUP PATH
Ctrl+C
