SIGNAL-SAFETY CAVEAT


Calling printf() and free() directly inside a signal handler is not considered strictly signal-safe in production code — both can, in rare cases, interact badly with whatever the main program was doing at the exact instant the signal arrived. For a teaching lab this is a fine way to see the mechanism in action; a hardened version would typically set a flag in the handler and do the actual cleanup back in main().
With Ctrl+C now routed through a real handler, the experiment gains a second lesson on top of the first: requesting memory, actually using it, and cleanly giving it back are three separate steps — and free -h can show you all three.
